 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Era of personal agents 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The long-promised personal AI assistant is finally arriving, with a suddenly crowded field of agents offering to run pieces of your everyday life.

 Why it matters: For years, tech's "personal assistants" were little more than voice-controlled search boxes. Rapid advances in AI are finally giving them the memory, judgment and autonomy to act on our behalf, Axios chief technology correspondent Ina Fried writes. Catch up fast: Chatbots answer questions. AI agents can actually do things — shopping, making calls, booking tickets, managing calendars and navigating the web.

 The model first took off in coding and other professional work. Now it's spilling into the far more intimate terrain of everyday life. 💥 Driving the news: A consumer-agent race has erupted almost overnight, with startups and tech giants pursuing the same basic idea — persistent digital proxies that know your life.

 Instinct has become buzzy by connecting to users' email, messages, screen, audio and location — then proactively texting or calling to help run their lives. It recently added the ability to make phone calls, and is now in talks for a $10 billion valuation . Grok Bot, launched by Elon Musk's SpaceXAI in August, gives persistent agents their own cloud computers, lets them sign into users' apps and keeps them working around the clock. Meta's Muse brought the concept to a broader consumer audience this month with a polished app, a customizable avatar and deep integration with Meta's ecosystem. Apple's Siri is finally getting its AI overhaul , bringing more context and capability to an assistant already built into hundreds of millions of devices. OpenAI is expected to enter the market soon after hiring the creator of OpenClaw — a breakout hit among AI power users earlier this year — to "drive the next generation of personal agents." 🔭 What we're watching: Becoming someone's default agent could be one of the most valuable relationships in consumer technology.

 People are likely to share far more with an AI that manages their lives than they did with a search engine. Read on . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. 🦾 Trump plans AI Force 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Via Truth Social 

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump said he's creating an "AI Force" modeled on the Space Force and will soon name a new AI czar.

 Why it matters: Trump is doubling down on accelerating AI development with limited regulation, even as the industry's biggest leaders call for stronger safety rules and as public fears grow, Axios' Andrew Pantazi writes. The latest: Trump offered few details on what this AI Force would actually do, what budget it would have or where it would be placed in the federal government.

 His Truth Social post said the government would "not in any way hinder or stifle" AI's growth but would look for "BAD" behavior using "our already existing Criminal and Civil Justice System." The intrigue: The AI czar position would revive a role that the White House didn't plan to fill six months ago.

 David Sacks left the formal AI and crypto czar job in March, though he remains influential as co-chair of the President's Council of Advisors on Science and Technology. Read on . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 3. 💰 Trump 2.0&#x27;s market 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Financial Modeling Prep . Chart: Erin Davis/Axios Visuals We're 20 months into President Trump's second term. Axios' Dan Primack reports on how the stock market's performance stacks up against past presidencies: 

 The market soared during the first 20 months of President Trump's second term, but slightly underperformed the same period of his first term.

 Why it matters: Trump considers the stock market to be a proxy for the economy, even though many economists disagree, Dan Primack writes. By the numbers: The S&amp;P 500 rose nearly 27.6% between inauguration day and this past Friday's close, compared to 28.5% during his first term.

 The gap at 20 months is closer than it was at the one-year mark , with Trump 2.0 leapfrogging former President Biden but still trailing former President Obama. Zoom in: Trump 2.0 trails Trump 1.0 on the Dow and Nasdaq, but is ahead on the Russell 3000.

