 6. 👀 3 GOP power players walk into a bar 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 It sounds like the start of an only-in-D.C. joke, Axios' Marc Caputo reports: 

 Three Republican power players walk into a bar for a freewheeling conversation. They unwittingly sit next to a liberal comic. She then skewers them in an Instagram post recounting their chat. Why it matters: It became no laughing matter for one of the Republicans — oilman Harry Sargeant — who's trying to improve his uneasy relations with the Trump administration, partly related to his business dealings in Venezuela. 

 His rapprochement efforts were complicated last month when senior administration officials became aware of the comic's social media post, in which she alleged that Sargeant spoke negatively about President Trump. 🥃 Zoom in: The episode unfolded Aug. 18 in the Bourbon Lounge of the Four Seasons hotel in D.C., where comic Suzanne Lambert was working on jokes for an upcoming set, she said in a post to her 495,000 Instagram followers that had 2 million views before she took it down.

 In the video, Lambert said she coincidentally wound up sitting next to Sargeant and noted a vibe shift when two other men walked in: Pete Marocco and Gentry Beach . Lambert said in her post that she was just "minding my business," but started to pay more attention when the men at the bar began "spouting off about Trump. I hear Trump, Trump, Trump, Trump." At one point, Lambert's post attributed comments to Sargeant that allegedly disparaged Trump and Republicans' midterm chances. Sargeant, through his attorneys, vigorously disputes that. 📱 Behind the scenes: Lambert's video quickly went viral, prompting Sargeant's attorneys to send her a cease and desist letter.

 Lambert took the post down. But by then, the post had circulated among Trump advisers. Secretary of State Marco Rubio was made aware of it, a source said. "Harry's got some problems, and what the White House saw didn't help," an administration source said of Sargeant. Keep reading . 

 
 
